1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,767
History wasn't lived as dates
on a timeline.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,800
It was a busy street
in Pompeii.

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,067
The cold of a Viking winter.

4
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:11,033
A choice made
when everything changed.

5
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:14,600
Here, we travel through
past civilizations,

6
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:17,167
exploring how people lived,
worked,

7
00:00:17,567 --> 00:00:19,467
and faced extraordinary events.

8
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,500
Our films combine
historical evidence,

9
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:26,033
clear storytelling,
and cinematic reconstructions.

10
00:00:26,567 --> 00:00:29,867
This is a journey
into the lives behind history.

11
00:00:30,833 --> 00:00:33,500
Our first destination: Pompeii.

12
00:00:34,133 --> 00:00:38,600
At first, lightweight white stones began
falling on Pompeii.

13
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:43,500
People sheltered beneath their roofs and
shut their doors, hoping to wait out the

14
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,700
strange storm. But the stones kept coming.

15
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:49,600
Roofs began to collapse
under their weight.

16
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:54,633
And by the following morning, the city
would face a danger that neither doors nor

17
00:00:54,633 --> 00:00:56,033
walls could keep out.

18
00:00:56,867 --> 00:00:59,567
It happened in the year 79 AD.

19
00:01:00,267 --> 00:01:05,400
To understand how one of the most famous
cities of ancient Rome was destroyed, we

20
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:10,533
need to reconstruct not only the eruption
of Mount Vesuvius, but also the decisions

21
00:01:10,533 --> 00:01:12,033
its residents had to make.

22
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,033
Should they leave or stay?

23
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:18,367
Run through streets where stones
were falling from the sky, or take

24
00:01:18,367 --> 00:01:20,400
shelter in a building that might collapse?

25
00:01:21,267 --> 00:01:26,000
We don't know what happened to every
family, and we won't invent anyone's final

26
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,400
conversations. But we do have letters
written by an eyewitness, marks

27
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:36,867
left on walls, objects found inside homes,
and layers of volcanic

28
00:01:36,867 --> 00:01:42,567
rock. Together, they let us piece together
the disaster, almost hour by

29
00:01:42,567 --> 00:01:48,167
hour. On an ordinary day, Pompeii
was a bustling trading city on the

30
00:01:48,167 --> 00:01:51,400
Bay of Naples. Carts
rolled through its streets.

31
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:57,600
Workshops made goods, bakeries ground
grain and baked bread, and stalls sold

32
00:01:57,600 --> 00:01:58,700
ready-to-eat food.

33
00:01:59,233 --> 00:02:04,200
People covered walls with announcements,
campaign slogans, and personal messages.

34
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:10,300
Some residents lived in spacious houses
with painted walls and interior gardens.

35
00:02:10,867 --> 00:02:15,567
Others worked in shops, rented modest
rooms, or lived in slavery.

36
00:02:15,933 --> 00:02:18,567
Pompeii wasn't just a city of wealthy
Romans.

37
00:02:19,067 --> 00:02:23,100
Its people had very different rights,
resources, and opportunities.

38
00:02:23,967 --> 00:02:27,067
Rising above the rooftops was Mount
Vesuvius.

39
00:02:27,533 --> 00:02:29,933
Fields and vineyards covered its slopes.

40
00:02:30,567 --> 00:02:35,833
Today we know it was a volcano, but to
most people living nearby, it did not look

41
00:02:35,833 --> 00:02:37,867
like a warning of imminent destruction.

42
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:43,733
Vesuvius had gone a very long time without
a major eruption, and memories of earlier

43
00:02:43,733 --> 00:02:47,467
disasters offered little clear
understanding of what was coming.

44
00:02:48,467 --> 00:02:51,867
The ground in this region,
however, was far from peaceful.

45
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:57,067
Seventeen years before Pompeii's
destruction, a powerful earthquake had

46
00:02:57,067 --> 00:02:58,700
damaged buildings and streets.

47
00:02:59,267 --> 00:03:03,700
The city was still being rebuilt,
and archaeologists can see traces of those

48
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:08,967
repairs today. More earthquakes followed,
but an earthquake alone did not tell

49
00:03:08,967 --> 00:03:12,700
residents that an eruption
of this magnitude was about to happen.

50
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:18,267
Sometime in late summer or fall of 79 AD,
an ordinary

51
00:03:18,267 --> 00:03:19,400
day changed.

52
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:22,567
Scholars still debate the exact date.

53
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:27,533
August twenty-fourth was long considered
the traditional date, but archaeological

54
00:03:27,533 --> 00:03:29,900
evidence also points toward the fall.

55
00:03:30,567 --> 00:03:34,233
For this story, the sequence of events
matters more than the season.

56
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,033
Sometime after noon, an enormous cloud
rose from Mount Vesuvius.

57
00:03:40,567 --> 00:03:44,600
It shot upward, then spread into wide
branches near the top.

58
00:03:45,133 --> 00:03:49,733
One of the main eyewitnesses, Pliny
the Younger, compared its shape to a pine

59
00:03:49,733 --> 00:03:53,700
tree. He was watching from Misenum,
on the other side of the bay.

60
00:03:54,033 --> 00:03:55,433
Not from Pompeii itself.

61
00:03:56,367 --> 00:04:00,200
What Pliny described was not ordinary
smoke from a fire.

62
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:06,600
Magma was rising inside the volcano,
and gases were forcing huge quantities of

63
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:09,800
scorching fragments and ash
into the atmosphere.

64
00:04:10,433 --> 00:04:14,600
A towering eruption column rose
tens of kilometers into the sky.

65
00:04:15,167 --> 00:04:19,233
The wind carried its contents
toward the towns south of Vesuvius.

66
00:04:20,067 --> 00:04:23,800
That wind determined which
danger would reach Pompeii first.

67
00:04:24,267 --> 00:04:29,667
The city began to be covered in pumice:
a light, porous rock formed from frothy

68
00:04:29,667 --> 00:04:30,867
volcanic material.

69
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:35,567
One piece can look almost harmless,
especially compared with glowing lava.

70
00:04:36,233 --> 00:04:41,800
But imagine not one little stone, but a
continuous rain of rock that goes on for

71
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:47,167
hours. Stones cover roads and courtyards,
piling up on roofs and at

72
00:04:47,167 --> 00:04:51,233
doorways. Each new layer makes the streets
harder to cross.

73
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:55,767
What you might brush off your shoulder
at first gradually becomes a serious

74
00:04:55,767 --> 00:05:00,833
obstruction. For someone inside a house,
the temptation is obvious:

75
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:06,600
wait it out. A roof offers some protection
from falling debris, and outside

76
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:08,633
it's difficult to see where you're going.

77
00:05:09,133 --> 00:05:11,667
But pumice keeps accumulating overhead.

78
00:05:12,233 --> 00:05:15,933
Eventually, its weight becomes dangerous
for the roof structure.

79
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:21,367
In Pompeii, archaeologists have found the
remains of people killed when roofs and

80
00:05:21,367 --> 00:05:22,367
walls collapsed.

81
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:26,667
Some residents did leave while an escape
route was still open.

82
00:05:27,167 --> 00:05:31,167
That's an important part of the story,
and one that movies often overlook.

83
00:05:31,667 --> 00:05:36,233
Pompeii was not a city where everyone
stayed and died at the same moment.

84
00:05:36,633 --> 00:05:41,333
We don't know exactly how many people
escaped, but evidence survives of people

85
00:05:41,333 --> 00:05:45,633
who lived through the eruption and of
later attempts to return for possessions.

86
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:51,267
Meanwhile, on the other side of the bay,
another dramatic event was unfolding.

87
00:05:51,933 --> 00:05:56,900
Pliny the Elder, the eyewitness's uncle,
commanded the Roman fleet at Misenum.

88
00:05:57,400 --> 00:06:01,600
At first, the unusual cloud interested him
as a subject of study.

89
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:04,633
He planned to sail closer and investigate
it.

90
00:06:05,033 --> 00:06:08,967
Then a woman living near the volcano sent
a request for help.

91
00:06:09,433 --> 00:06:10,600
His mission changed.

92
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:15,967
Pliny ordered ships to put to sea and
headed toward a place others were trying

93
00:06:15,967 --> 00:06:21,300
to escape. The closer the vessels came
to shore, the thicker the falling ash

94
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:24,100
became, and the more
debris struck the water.

95
00:06:24,700 --> 00:06:26,967
They couldn't reach their intended
destination.

96
00:06:28,033 --> 00:06:31,667
Eventually, Pliny arrived at Stabiae,
where his acquaintance

97
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:33,167
Pomponianus was staying.

98
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:37,967
This was not a rescue directly
from the city gates of Pompeii.

99
00:06:38,367 --> 00:06:40,967
The letter describes another
part of the coastline.

100
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:46,433
But it reveals the larger situation:
people were trying to escape by land and

101
00:06:46,433 --> 00:06:50,433
by sea, while the eruption was steadily
cutting off possible routes.

102
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:54,867
By evening, Pompeii was far darker than
usual.

103
00:06:55,367 --> 00:06:58,767
Almost no sunlight could penetrate
the volcanic cloud.

104
00:06:59,233 --> 00:07:02,567
The streets were buried beneath mountains
of pumice.

105
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,367
Familiar routes through the city
had become difficult to navigate.

106
00:07:06,867 --> 00:07:11,200
With every step, it grew harder to avoid
stumbling over stones.

107
00:07:12,167 --> 00:07:14,733
And there was another danger: earthquakes.

108
00:07:15,633 --> 00:07:19,167
As the eruption continued, the shaking
intensified.

109
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:24,500
Buildings already weakened by earlier
damage and the weight of fallen material

110
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:30,167
could collapse. A study published in 2024
suggests that some residents

111
00:07:30,167 --> 00:07:33,567
may have died because
of earthquake-related building collapses

112
00:07:33,567 --> 00:07:38,500
during the eruption, rather than directly
from volcanic ash or extreme heat.

113
00:07:39,367 --> 00:07:41,000
It was a terrifying choice.

114
00:07:41,533 --> 00:07:44,167
Outside, stones were falling from the sky.

115
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:46,800
Inside, the ceiling could come down.

116
00:07:47,133 --> 00:07:51,767
And no one could know how long it
would last: another hour, or another week.

117
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:56,467
There were no modern alerts, no
communication networks, and no maps

118
00:07:56,467 --> 00:07:57,533
showing safe zones.

119
00:07:57,933 --> 00:08:02,033
People had to make decisions based on what
they could see and hear around them.

120
00:08:02,767 --> 00:08:07,533
Pliny the Younger recorded that, across
the bay, people called out to locate their

121
00:08:07,533 --> 00:08:11,133
relatives because darkness had swallowed
everything around them.

122
00:08:11,467 --> 00:08:13,533
He himself left town with his mother.

123
00:08:13,933 --> 00:08:18,867
According to his account, the ground shook
so violently that carts would not stay in

124
00:08:18,867 --> 00:08:21,833
place, even with stones
wedged beneath their wheels.

125
00:08:22,633 --> 00:08:27,300
While pumice continued to rain down
on Pompeii, the neighboring city of

126
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:30,267
Herculaneum faced a different
sequence of events.

127
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:35,133
At first, the wind carried most
of the falling stones away from it.

128
00:08:35,333 --> 00:08:37,400
But that did not mean it was safe.

129
00:08:38,067 --> 00:08:43,700
During the night, pyroclastic flows rushed
down the slopes of Vesuvius: mixtures of

130
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:49,233
hot gas, ash, and rock fragments moving
at tremendous speed close to the ground.

131
00:08:50,067 --> 00:08:54,967
These flows are nothing like a slow river
of lava that you might have time to walk

132
00:08:54,967 --> 00:09:00,600
around. They spread rapidly, pour into
low-lying areas, and destroy what lies

133
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:05,233
in their path. Herculaneum was struck
by this danger before Pompeii.

134
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:09,000
Its buildings and streets were buried
beneath a thick layer of

135
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:10,067
volcanic deposits.

136
00:09:10,967 --> 00:09:14,867
Pompeii, meanwhile, was still
enduring the rain of stones.

137
00:09:15,533 --> 00:09:17,433
It continued for many hours.

138
00:09:18,033 --> 00:09:22,567
By the following morning, around three
meters of pumice had accumulated in some

139
00:09:22,567 --> 00:09:27,533
places. Picture the height of the ceiling
in an ordinary room, and imagine almost

140
00:09:27,533 --> 00:09:30,433
that much loose rock
piled up in the streets.

141
00:09:30,967 --> 00:09:35,233
Opening a door, climbing out of a
ground-floor room, or crossing a mound of

142
00:09:35,233 --> 00:09:37,933
debris had become extraordinarily
difficult.

143
00:09:38,667 --> 00:09:41,567
Then the intensity of the falling stones
changed.

144
00:09:42,100 --> 00:09:45,033
For a short time, it may have seemed
that the worst was over.

145
00:09:45,433 --> 00:09:50,333
But the layers of volcanic deposits tell
scientists this was not the end of the

146
00:09:50,333 --> 00:09:53,567
eruption. Conditions above
Vesuvius were changing.

147
00:09:54,067 --> 00:09:58,567
Parts of the enormous eruption column
collapsed, sending new pyroclastic flows

148
00:09:58,567 --> 00:10:00,067
racing across the ground.

149
00:10:00,700 --> 00:10:03,500
One of them reached Pompeii the following
morning.

150
00:10:04,033 --> 00:10:09,267
A powerful mixture of volcanic gases, fine
ash, and hot rock fragments

151
00:10:09,267 --> 00:10:12,533
swept into places where people had tried
to hide.

152
00:10:12,967 --> 00:10:17,967
It moved far faster than a human could
run, and it didn't need an open door to

153
00:10:17,967 --> 00:10:20,300
turn a house into a deadly trap.

154
00:10:21,100 --> 00:10:25,833
This is where the familiar movie image
of Pompeii's destruction falls apart.

155
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:30,200
The city was not simply swallowed
by a flood of glowing red lava.

156
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:35,400
The main destructive forces were falling
pumice, collapsing buildings, earthquakes,

157
00:10:35,967 --> 00:10:37,367
and pyroclastic flows.

158
00:10:37,900 --> 00:10:42,367
Different people died in different
circumstances, and scientists are still

159
00:10:42,367 --> 00:10:44,967
working to understand the precise
mechanisms.

160
00:10:45,767 --> 00:10:50,733
One study estimated that a deadly flow
reaching Pompeii may have affected the

161
00:10:50,733 --> 00:10:53,167
city for around ten to twenty minutes.

162
00:10:53,767 --> 00:10:58,500
For people trapped in a cloud filled with
ash they could inhale, that was far too

163
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:03,933
long. Other studies and museum accounts
emphasize the effects of intense heat.

164
00:11:04,367 --> 00:11:09,033
We cannot determine the exact cause of
death of every person whose remains have

165
00:11:09,033 --> 00:11:14,333
been found. What is clear is that once
these flows arrived, those still

166
00:11:14,333 --> 00:11:17,300
inside the city had almost
no chance of escaping.

167
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:23,167
Places where homes, streets, workshops,
and gardens had stood only hours earlier

168
00:11:23,167 --> 00:11:25,300
were covered by volcanic deposits.

169
00:11:25,833 --> 00:11:28,667
The ancient city we know today was buried.

170
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:32,367
Pliny the Elder never
returned from his voyage.

171
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:34,267
He died at Stabiae.

172
00:11:34,833 --> 00:11:40,100
Years later, his nephew described both
that journey and his own escape in letters

173
00:11:40,100 --> 00:11:41,800
to the historian Tacitus.

174
00:11:42,333 --> 00:11:47,267
We know an extraordinary amount about this
disaster because that human eyewitness

175
00:11:47,267 --> 00:11:51,700
account survived. But the letters
were written after the eruption, so their

176
00:11:51,700 --> 00:11:54,733
details have to be compared
with the archaeological evidence.

177
00:11:55,767 --> 00:11:59,033
Life in the region did not end forever
after the disaster.

178
00:11:59,533 --> 00:12:00,833
Some residents survived.

179
00:12:01,467 --> 00:12:05,033
Others tried to return and recover
whatever belongings were left.

180
00:12:05,533 --> 00:12:08,300
Officials under Emperor Titus
dealt with the aftermath.

181
00:12:08,933 --> 00:12:12,767
People continued searching the site
of Pompeii for building materials and

182
00:12:12,767 --> 00:12:17,267
possessions. But the city as it had once
functioned was never restored.

183
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:22,867
Over time, soil and new layers of human
activity covered the ruins.

184
00:12:23,433 --> 00:12:25,833
The memory of the city gradually faded.

185
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:30,067
Systematic excavations began
here in the eighteenth century.

186
00:12:30,567 --> 00:12:35,433
Researchers went on to discover not only
walls and household objects, but also

187
00:12:35,433 --> 00:12:38,233
traces of the people
caught in the catastrophe.

188
00:12:38,967 --> 00:12:43,433
One of Pompeii's most famous features
is the plaster casts of the victims.

189
00:12:44,233 --> 00:12:48,833
These are not people who turned to stone,
as photographs sometimes make them appear.

190
00:12:49,333 --> 00:12:54,600
Their bodies decomposed over time, leaving
hollow spaces in the hardened volcanic

191
00:12:54,600 --> 00:13:00,067
material. In the nineteenth century,
archaeologist Giuseppe Fiorelli developed

192
00:13:00,067 --> 00:13:05,033
a method of pouring plaster into those
spaces, recreating the shapes of bodies

193
00:13:05,033 --> 00:13:06,967
and even the folds of clothing.

194
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:11,367
The remains of more than a thousand
victims have been found.

195
00:13:11,767 --> 00:13:16,833
But that is not a complete death toll, and
certainly not a count of Pompeii's entire

196
00:13:16,833 --> 00:13:22,033
population. Archaeologists have not
investigated every location, and many

197
00:13:22,033 --> 00:13:23,633
people managed to leave the city.

198
00:13:24,167 --> 00:13:29,233
What survives tells us about individual
human lives, but it cannot give us a

199
00:13:29,233 --> 00:13:33,400
complete record of every resident
and every final decision they made.

200
00:13:34,100 --> 00:13:37,333
And that is what makes Pompeii so
extraordinary.

201
00:13:37,933 --> 00:13:42,967
Almost two thousand years separate us from
its inhabitants, yet we can still see a

202
00:13:42,967 --> 00:13:47,767
street, a bakery, a wall covered
in writing, and dishes used for meals.

203
00:13:48,167 --> 00:13:52,600
We can glimpse a world where people made
plans for tomorrow, repaired their homes,

204
00:13:52,967 --> 00:13:54,167
worked, and argued.

205
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:59,167
They had no idea that their ordinary day
would become a chapter in history.

206
00:13:59,900 --> 00:14:05,500
Today, almost everyone knows Pompeii
as the city destroyed by Mount Vesuvius.

207
00:14:05,933 --> 00:14:11,167
But to the people living there in 79 AD,
it was, above all, home.

208
00:14:11,833 --> 00:14:16,233
And when that enormous cloud rose above
the mountain, they were not facing an

209
00:14:16,233 --> 00:14:19,533
abstract historical question about how
volcanoes work.

210
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:24,900
They faced one immediate, practical
question: would they make it out alive?

211
00:14:25,800 --> 00:14:31,100
Some did. Others remained beneath roofs
that could no longer bear the weight of

212
00:14:31,100 --> 00:14:36,600
the stones. Still others survived the long
night, only to be trapped when the

213
00:14:36,600 --> 00:14:38,200
scorching cloud arrived.

214
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:44,000
The ash buried the city, but it preserved
traces of the lives lived there.

215
00:14:44,500 --> 00:14:49,967
And today, whenever archaeologists uncover
another street or room, we learn not

216
00:14:49,967 --> 00:14:52,100
only about Pompeii's final day.

217
00:14:52,467 --> 00:14:56,633
We learn about the thousands of ordinary
days that came before it.
